WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
Thailand, the late 1980s.

2
00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.250
Loggers are moving into the forests.

3
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.600
Monks wrap the oldest
trees in orange robes.

4
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.120
They ordain them, as if
each tree were a monk.

5
00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.480
Myanmar, 2007.

6
00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:15.930
Monks march.

7
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.690
Their alms bowls are turned upside down.

8
00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.500
Buddhism is often seen as inward-looking.

9
00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.760
Yet here are monks taking
on loggers and soldiers.

10
00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.660
So can a Buddhist ordain a
tree, march against an army,

11
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.710
or ever support a war?

12
00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.870
In this mission, you'll see why some
Buddhists treat activism as part of their

13
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.150
practice, not a break from it.

14
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.930
Then you'll weigh up whether social
engagement should be central to the Buddhist

15
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:41.950
path.

16
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.680
Your mission: explain how
far Buddhist action can go,

17
00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.200
from trees to protest to war.
